What changed and why:

- **GCD replaced by `async` and `@concurrent`.** `load()` is `async`, so it runs on the main actor. `@concurrent` moves the blocking read and parse onto the concurrent pool. This is the Swift 6.2 replacement for `DispatchQueue.global().async` followed by `DispatchQueue.main.async`. After the `await`, execution resumes on the main actor, so no manual hop back is needed.
- **No `@MainActor` annotation.** Default isolation already makes `FeedModel` main-actor isolated. `@concurrent` implies `nonisolated`, so the static function doesn't touch main-actor state.
- **`try!` removed.** A bad read no longer crashes the app. Errors go into `error`, which the UI can show.
- **No `self` capture.** The old code captured `self` in an escaping closure. This version has no closure, so there is no retain cycle and no Sendable warning.
- **`@Observable` replaces `ObservableObject` and `@Published`.** Views then only re-render for the properties they read. If you must support OS versions older than iOS 17 or macOS 14, keep `ObservableObject` and `@Published`. The concurrency changes work the same way either way.
