## `shot.js`

```js
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

(async () => {
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const page = await browser.newPage({ viewport: { width: 1280, height: 800 } });

  await page.goto('http://localhost:3000/login');

  await page.locator('input[type="email"], input[name="email"]').first().fill('demo@example.com');
  await page.locator('input[type="password"], input[name="password"]').first().fill('demo');
  await page.locator('button[type="submit"]').first().click();

  // Wait until we've left the login page (login succeeded)
  await page.waitForURL(url => !url.pathname.startsWith('/login'), { timeout: 10000 });

  await page.goto('http://localhost:3000/dashboard');
  await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');

  await page.screenshot({ path: 'dash.png', fullPage: true });
  await browser.close();
  console.log('Saved dash.png');
})().catch(err => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

## Run

```bash
node shot.js
```

## Notes

- The selectors assume a standard form with an email input, a password input, and a submit button. If your form differs, change them. For example, with labels you could use `page.getByLabel('Email').fill(...)`, or with a custom button `page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click()`.
- If the login fails, the `waitForURL` step will time out after 10 seconds with an error. To debug, change `chromium.launch()` to `chromium.launch({ headless: false })` to watch it run.
- If the dashboard loads data slowly after `networkidle`, add `await page.waitForTimeout(1000);` before the screenshot.

